From 5e507be2cd88e7dd1e4349e5e00d828b2837694c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 08:33:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] PicoMEM 1.x : Fixes, the Sound Blaster during disk access,
 multi sector disk read

PicoMEM 1.x
- Restore CYW43_PIO_CLOCK_DIV_INT=4 in the "< 1.5" block and the RP2040's
  1.20V. Without them a 1.x build never lights the LED and the PC hangs
  after the BIOS banner.
- The fake address of a DMA (AEN) cycle has A10 set (0x0FFFFF0F4). With it
  clear, a DMA cycle whose low address byte was in xx00-xx0F or xx80-xx8F
  was decoded as a write to the 8237 registers. Noted in pm_isa_rp2350.h
  but not applied there (not tested).

Sound Blaster with the emulated DMA (USE_HWDMA=0)
- The SB worker runs with the mixer during disk access: in the disk loop,
  in the PC command waits of an Int 13h (pm_pccmd_wait_hook, core 0 only),
  and in the SD driver's token and ready waits.
- 512 Bytes DMA copy (was 128) and a 2048 samples FIFO. The SB starts
  playing at 1024 samples, the other FIFOs keep 512 (start_thr).
- A sound shorter than the start level is played when its DMA ends.
- The block IRQ is sent when the block's bytes are read from the PC, not
  when its time is up. Several pending blocks send one IRQ each.
- The rest of the last copy is dropped when the PC programs the DMA again,
  so it is not counted as part of the next transfer.

Disk read to the PC RAM
- The sectors of an Int 13h read are read in runs (one f_read, so one SD
  multi block command) into a Pico buffer, then copied by the PC from the
  BIOS RAM transfer buffer with one command. The next run is read while the
  PC copies the previous one.
- The runs are 1, 2 then 4 sectors: the first read is the only one the PC
  waits for, and a run of n is read during the copy of n/2.
- A destination that is not all PC RAM (emulated RAM, PSRAM or EMS) still
  uses the sector loop.
- On a 16MHz 286 (PicoMEM 1.x, Pico W) a sector is ~650us of PC copy against
  ~270us of SD read. The disk read rate goes from 685 to 755 KB/s, and from
  345 to 380 KB/s with half the CPU busy: 96% of what the 8 bit ISA copy
  allows. Not tested on a PicoMEM 2.

The PC's copy during a disk access
- The disk code waits for the PC to end a copy with no time limit, as
  PC_End does, and stops only on a PC reset. The PC copies with its
  interrupts enabled, so an interrupt handler can hold it for more than the
  120 ms (20 + 100) the wait allowed - a program drawing from the timer
  interrupt does - and the sector loops do not test PC_MemCopyW_512b's
  result: the sector was left out of the read or the write, with no error.

